BACKGROUND = "<h2>Background of the Invention</h2>\n" + "".join([
 P("B01", "Information systems answer questions in two principal ways. Retrieval systems — databases, document stores and search engines — return stored rows or documents, but do not establish which specific assertion supports an answer or how reliable that assertion is. Generative language models produce fluent text by statistical next-token prediction and have no native mechanism to distinguish a well-supported answer from a plausible one, because both are produced by the same generative process. Retrieval-augmented generation attaches retrieved documents to a generative model, but the generated text remains unconstrained by them."),
 P("B02", "The consequences are well known. A fabricated statement is indistinguishable in form from a supported one; a citation names a source without a verifiable binding between the sentence and the evidence; confidence, where reported at all, is a single number that cannot distinguish strong evidence from thin evidence or one source repeated from many independent sources; contradictions between sources go undetected; and a correction made in one place does not propagate to the answers that relied on the corrected material."),
 P("B03", "Knowledge stored as text is also entangled with the language in which it was written. A corpus in six languages is in effect six corpora: a query must be translated to reach material in another language, a correction must be made once per language, and an error introduced in translation is indistinguishable afterwards from an error in the source."),
 P("B04", "Sharing knowledge across organizational boundaries presents further problems. A record received from another system is typically trusted or rejected wholesale rather than re-validated against its certification; removing a single record from an immutable or append-only store conflicts with the store’s integrity guarantees; and safety and access rules are commonly applied as filters around a result, which a code path can bypass, rather than carried within each record. As automated agents begin to act on users’ behalf, the agents and the knowledge systems they use can themselves be targeted — through injected instructions, stolen credentials or altered logs."),
 P("B05", "Conventional natural-language and speech technology, on which question answering in many languages depends, either encodes grammar, pronunciation and prosody in manually authored rules, dictionaries and language packs, which are brittle and language-specific, or trains neural models top-down on tokenized text without grounding in the acoustic and communicative foundations from which human language develops, and without exploiting the structural transfer by which a first language accelerates the acquisition of later ones."),
 P("B06", "There exists a need for a knowledge and query system in which (a) every assertion is an atomic, content-identified record carrying an evidence-based confidence, an authority certification, a verifiable provenance and its own safety classification; (b) queries evaluate structure and trust together; (c) every output is composed only from verified records and is cited or refused; (d) knowledge is independent of the language in which it arrived; (e) records are re-validated when they cross boundaries and can be erased individually without weakening the store’s integrity; (f) a single safety constraint governs every subsystem and every agent action; and (g) language and speech, identity, media and agent capabilities are built on that core and inherit its guarantees rather than weakening them."),
])

SUMMARY = """<h2>Summary of the Invention</h2>
  <p class="add">QueryBook is a deterministic, provenance-tracked federated knowledge and query system. Its core, described in Part A (FIG. 1–FIG. 22), comprises: (1) the <b>Fact Unit</b>, an immutable record encoding exactly one assertion as a nucleus of subject, predicate, object and polarity, identified by a semantic fingerprint derived from its content and carrying in its envelope an evidence-based confidence, an authority certification, a signed provenance reference, temporal anchors, typed links and an in-record safety classification and access-control list; (2) the <b>UFCS substrate</b>, a content-addressed fact graph, ontology mesh and append-only provenance ledger, with a serialized packet form and fingerprint-indexed block storage that stores identical meaning once; (3) a <b>certification authority hierarchy</b> under which each record inherits a trust ceiling from its issuing authority; (4) the <b>Federated Query Language (FQL)</b>, which evaluates structural patterns and trust thresholds together during traversal, excludes contradicted records and binds every result to a response provenance hash; (5) <b>verification</b> of any machine-generated text against certified records as verified, contradicted or unknown; (6) <b>grounded response generation</b> under a directive fixed before generation, with a vocabulary constrained to the contributing records, which cites or refuses; (7) <b>federation</b> with re-validation at every boundary and <b>fact-level erasure</b> by destruction of an external key; (8) the <b>Prime Directive</b>, a Domain-0 safety plane governing every answer, output and scheduled action, with a rule forbidding the withholding of entitled information as a means of avoiding a harm determination; and (9) <b>governed user-owned agents</b>, a layered security architecture, deployment topologies over an invariant common layer, language-independent regeneration, and provenance-carrying transport over TCP/IP.</p>
  <p class="add">Because a record’s identity is derived from its meaning rather than from its wording, the same assertion arriving in different languages or from different sources converges on one record with several provenance branches; corroboration, contradiction and duplication are detected at ingestion; and a correction is made once. Because generation draws only on the records a query returned, an ungrounded assertion is structurally unavailable to the system rather than merely discouraged, and identical inputs in an identical context produce identical output.</p>
  <p class="add">On that core the invention provides subsystems and embodiments, each inheriting the core’s guarantees: in Part B, the integral language and speech subsystem — the Language Expression Layer with its Sub-Language Priming Layer, one-way Transition Gate, multilingual Delta Acquisition, Speech Output Pipeline and co-grounding with FQL and UFCS, together with deterministic, reduced-to-practice pronunciation, speech, translation and quality-control embodiments; in Part C, voiceprint and multimodal identity linking, an independent integrity-audit service, a paralinguistic and pragmatic interpretation layer, and a lecture-query mode; and in Part D, deterministic multilingual acquisition, dialect parameter clusters, grounded scene compilation, key-safe service mediation, edition packaging, a version-independent store, cited question-and-answer and other application surfaces, meaning-based handling of pictures, sound and video, and agent-era extensions for arbitrary-artifact ingestion, on-chain certification, agent protection and deception, a tamper-evident security record, agent interoperability and framed transport.</p>
  <p class="add">No large language model asserts a fact anywhere in the system. A large language model may be used only in non-asserting roles — to phrase an answer over records the system has already verified, or to propose hypotheses for exploration — and anything it proposes is admitted only after independent verification against the deterministic store; its output, where retained, is marked as externally generated and is inadmissible as a grounding source.</p>
"""

